Message-ID: <NnK6Qu-I1Xo.C-6NN6a59TIJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <NnK6Qu-I1Xo.xP4NVvukWU4J@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
References: <NnK6Qu-I1Xo.xP4NVvukWU4J@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/NnK6Qu-I1Xo/m/C-6NN6a59TIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi:

Here's an example - I have no idea how close it is to your situation
(and that's a hint!).

DF <- data.frame(f1 = sample(letters[1:3], 200, replace = TRUE),
                          f2 = sample(LETTERS[1:3], 200, replace = TRUE),
                           y = rpois(200, 6))
library('plyr')
library('reshape2')
# Sort by decreasing values of A
arrange(dcast(DF, f1 ~ f2, value_var = 'y', fun = length), desc(A))
  f1  A  B  C
1  c 24 22 22
2  b 22 19 24
3  a 21 19 27
# If you need a tiebreaker, here's an example on B, then C
arrange(dcast(DF, f1 ~ f2, value_var = 'y', fun = length), desc(B), desc(C))
  f1  A  B  C
1  c 24 22 22
2  a 21 19 27
3  b 22 19 24

arrange() is in the plyr package, and is a replacement function of
sorts for order() with respect to columns of a data frame.

HTH,
Dennis

